<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Grivas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Georgios Grivas, Digenis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Grivas was a Cypriot-born Greek army officer who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoka">EOKA</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign against British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, from 1955 to 1959 under the code name Digenis. He opposed the independence settlement, which ruled out union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and in 1971 he returned secretly to Cyprus to found EOKA-B against President Makarios; he died in hiding in 1974.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-grivas.jpg" alt="George Grivas" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgios_Grivas_1967.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Grivas was born at Trikomo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> to Greek Cypriot parents and studied at the Pancyprian Gymnasium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> from 1909 to 1915. In 1916 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, took Greek citizenship and entered the Hellenic Military Academy, completing his studies at the École Militaire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Commissioned in 1919, he served with the 10th Division in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War</a>, advancing to Eskişehir and fighting at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sakarya">Battle of Sakarya</a>, and was decorated for bravery. He rose to major in 1935 and in 1938 married Vasiliki Deka.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the Second World War Grivas was chief of staff of the 2nd Division on the Albanian front, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> occupation he founded Organisation X, a group of army officers that spied for the Allied powers and later fought EAM/<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-peoples-liberation-army">ELAS</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in December 1944. Retiring in 1946, he took the oath of Enosis with Archbishop Makarios III, landed secretly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in October 1954 and opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoka">EOKA</a> campaign on 1 April 1955 with bombings and a declaration signed DIGENIS. He escaped British forces at Spilia in December 1955 and at Kykkos in May 1956, and the colonial government offered £10,000 for his capture.</p><p>After the Zurich-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> agreements of 1959 he reluctantly ordered a ceasefire and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where he was promoted general. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 1964, he commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> and the Greek division; Operation Gronthos at Kofinou and Agios Theodoros on 15 November 1967 left 27 dead, mostly Turkish Cypriots, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s ultimatum led to his recall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1968–1969 he took part in a failed plot against the Greek junta. He returned secretly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> on 31 August 1971 and formed EOKA B to force Makarios to adopt union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and the struggle grew into civil strife among Greek Cypriots. He died of heart failure in a house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limassol">Limassol</a> on 27 January 1974 and was buried two days later before tens of thousands of mourners, though the Makarios government boycotted the funeral. EOKA B then came under the control of Dimitrios Ioannidis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, whose coup of 15 July 1974 against Makarios was followed by the Turkish invasion on 20 July.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georgios Grivas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1955 the EOKA guerrilla movement under <b>George Grivas</b>, backed by Archbishop Makarios III, fought British rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-cyprus">British Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limassol">Limassol</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.17,33.37&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Nicosia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of George Grivas"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5235 4555h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5235 4555h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.17,33.37&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
